<p>Chain-driven accumulation examples come from Hytrol's <a href="https://hytrol.com/products/pallet-handling/chain-roller-conveyor/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">chain driven live roller line</a>, which Hytrol describes as conveyors that use chain drives to transport or accumulate pallets. For chain-drive details, see the <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR conveyor guide</a>.</p>
<h2 id="tech-heading">Four Live Roller Drive Technologies</h2>
<h3>Belt-Driven Live Roller (BDLR)</h3>
<p>BDLR conveyors use a continuous belt running beneath the rollers. Pressure rollers hold the belt against the underside of the carrying rollers, and friction turns them. A single AC gearmotor drives the belt through a drive pulley, with a take-up to maintain tension. Drive pressure can be adjusted: Hytrol's 190-ACC uses knurled nuts to set pressure-roller contact for minimum-pressure accumulation.</p>
<p>BDLR is a common base for <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/powered/">powered roller conveyor</a> installations. With pneumatic zone controls, BDLR supports zero-pressure <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation</a>. In Hytrol's ABEZ, each zone has a pressure frame to apply and remove drive and a pneumatic brake.</p>
<h3>Line-Shaft</h3>
<p>A line-shaft conveyor uses a continuously rotating steel shaft along the conveyor length. Drive spools on the shaft connect to each roller through a polyurethane O-ring band. An AC gearmotor turns the shaft.</p>
<p>The O-ring connection provides a built-in slip clutch: if a product stops, the spool slips on the shaft rather than stalling the motor. This passive slip provides light <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation</a> without active zone control. Line-shaft works well with pop-up <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">transfer</a> sections because rollers are driven individually. For zero-pressure accumulation, zone brakes are added: Hytrol's 190-NSPEZ uses air bags and brake pads in each zone.</p>
<h3>Chain-Driven Live Roller (CDLR)</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR conveyors</a> use roller chain and sprockets to positively drive all rollers, with no slip at any load. CDLR is the standard live roller technology for <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">pallet conveyor systems</a> and heavy industrial applications. Capacity rises with roller size and support spacing: Hytrol's 36-CRRH, with 3-1/2-inch by 0.300-inch wall rollers, is rated at 2,000 lbs per linear foot with supports on 5-foot centers. Zoned CDLR models put a gearmotor in each zone for zero-pressure accumulation.</p>
<h3>Motor Driven Roller (MDR)</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a> place a low-voltage brushless DC motor inside one roller per zone. That motor roller drives the other rollers in the zone through bands or belts, creating an independent zone. MDR provides built-in zero-pressure accumulation without pneumatics, a line shaft, or a long drive belt, and is a common choice for new zone-controlled lines in distribution and fulfillment. Load per zone is limited by motor roller torque and roller rating, so confirm the zone rating for your heaviest product.</p>
