<p>Zone methods reflect Hytrol's published installation manuals for its ABEZ, 190-NSPEZ, and CREZD models. Speed and load ratings vary by model, so compare data sheets for the models quoted. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for cost factors.</p>
<h2 id="decision-heading">Which Live Roller Technology Do You Need?</h2>
<h3>Start with Load</h3>
<p>Load is the first filter. Compare your heaviest product, and your heaviest load per foot when products queue, against each model's per-foot and per-roller rating. Loaded pallets and drums point to <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR</a>. Cases, cartons, and totes fit BDLR, line-shaft, or MDR, subject to each model's rating.</p>
<h3>Do You Need Accumulation?</h3>
<p>If yes, <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR</a> provides <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">zero-pressure accumulation</a> without compressed air. BDLR with pneumatic zones is the traditional alternative, and BDLR with low drive pressure gives minimum-pressure accumulation. Line-shaft provides light passive accumulation through O-ring slip, or zero-pressure with zone brakes. CDLR accumulates with a gearmotor per zone.</p>
<h3>Is Noise a Constraint?</h3>
<p>Noise depends on speed, load, drive type, and building acoustics. MDR zones stop when no product is present, while continuous-run drives keep moving. If conveyors run through occupied work areas, ask each supplier for measured sound levels at your speed and load, and compare against your plant's noise limits.</p>
<h3>Do You Need Diverting?</h3>
<p>Line-shaft conveyors work well with pop-up <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">transfer</a> diverters because rollers are driven individually through their own O-rings. BDLR and MDR also work with transfers but need different zone control approaches at the divert point.</p>
<h3>Budget: Upfront vs Total Cost</h3>
<p>Continuous-run BDLR and line-shaft transport sections usually cost less per foot upfront. MDR and CDLR cost more, for different reasons: MDR adds a motor roller and controller per zone, and CDLR adds heavier rollers, frames, and chain. MDR's on-demand zones and lack of compressed air can lower operating cost. Compare quotes on total cost over the conveyor's life, including compressed air, energy, and wear parts. The <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> breaks down these factors.</p>
<h3>Mixed Systems</h3>
<p>Many <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automated conveyor systems</a> combine multiple live roller technologies. A typical <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging line</a> might use MDR for accumulation zones, BDLR for transport sections, <a href="/conveyor-types/pallet-conveyors/">CDLR for pallet handling</a>, and <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/gravity/">gravity roller</a> for take-away and manual stations. Matching frame and roller dimensions across the technologies keeps product transitions clean at section boundaries.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Live Roller Applications</h2>
<p><strong>End-of-line packaging:</strong> BDLR and MDR transport sealed cases between case sealers, labelers, and palletizers. <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">Accumulation zones</a> buffer cases before the palletizer. CDLR handles loaded pallets between the palletizer and stretch wrapper.</p>
<p><strong>Distribution and fulfillment:</strong> MDR is common in new distribution center installations for tote and carton transport with zero-pressure accumulation. Line-shaft conveyors remain common in existing systems and diverter-intensive layouts.</p>
<p><strong><a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">Food and beverage:</a></strong> Stainless steel live roller and MDR conveyors handle cased goods in <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary</a> and washdown environments. Open-frame construction and sealed bearings support cleaning protocols.</p>
<p><strong>Pallet handling:</strong> <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR</a> is the usual live roller technology for full pallet loads. <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">Pallet conveyor systems</a> use CDLR for transport, right-angle chain <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">transfers</a> for routing, and <a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-reciprocating/">VRCs</a> for vertical transport.</p>
<p><strong>Assembly and manufacturing:</strong> Live roller conveyors transport work-in-process between assembly stations. <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">Indexing configurations</a> stop and start products at each station for manual or robotic operations.</p>
