<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a live roller conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Any <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyor</a> where rollers are actively powered. The three conventional technologies are BDLR (belt-driven), line-shaft, and <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR</a> (chain-driven). <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR</a> (motor inside the roller) is a newer fourth approach.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is the difference between CDLR and BDLR?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>CDLR drives every roller positively with roller chain and sprockets, so rollers keep turning under heavy or slippery loads. It is the usual choice for pallets, drums, and heavy unit loads, with capacity rated per foot: Hytrol's 26-CRR is rated at 300 lbs per foot on 10-foot supports or 1,000 lbs per foot on 5-foot supports. BDLR drives rollers by friction from a belt held against their undersides and suits cases, cartons, and totes. Both come in zero-pressure versions: pneumatic zones for BDLR, a gearmotor per zone for CDLR.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">BDLR vs line-shaft, what is the difference?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>BDLR uses a belt held against the rollers by pressure rollers, with adjustable drive force. Line-shaft uses a rotating shaft with spools and an O-ring to each roller. Line-shaft O-rings slip when product is held, which allows light accumulation, and individually driven rollers suit pop-up transfer diverting. See <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/powered/">powered roller conveyors</a> for details.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">Should I choose MDR or belt-driven live roller?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>MDR for built-in zone control and zero-pressure accumulation without compressed air, when product weight is within the motor roller and zone rating. BDLR for continuous transport, heavier cases beyond the MDR zone rating, or lower upfront cost. Compare total cost over the conveyor's life, including compressed air, energy, and wear parts.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">What is belt driven live roller used for?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>BDLR transports cases, cartons, and totes at controlled speed in <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging lines</a> and distribution centers. With pneumatic zones it provides zero-pressure <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation</a>. With low drive pressure it provides minimum-pressure accumulation, which Hytrol states can bring backpressure to 2% of the live load.</p>

</div>
</div>
